  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1153 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nIsaias continued to intensify Wednesday evening. Per NHC, Isaias\nhas just reached hurricane strength.\n\nOverall, little has changed from the 4 pm / 21Z advisory\nregarding potential impacts for the Midsouth. These impacts will\nprimarily occur between sunrise Saturday and midnight Saturday\nevening. The system will accelerate northward through the day\nSaturday, which will limit, but not preclude flooding concerns.\nAt this time, 1-3 inches appears likely, with half or more of\nthis rainfall occurring Saturday morning. With much of the\nMidsouth in D2 to D4 drought, but dry ground will not completely\nmitigate a flooding concern if rain rates are high. A Flood Watch\nis not planned for this forecast cycle but may be considered in\nfuture forecast cycles.\n\nWe are just outside the window for high res-CAM guidance, but\ndeterministic runs of the GFS and the REFS ensemble mean indicate\na potential for 35+ mph gusts over northeast MS and the TN River\nSaturday afternoon. Areas west of the MS River could see gusts in\nexcess of 30 mph Saturday afternoon, given the relatively flat\nland surface. Although a brief tornado cannot be ruled out, the\nright/eastern semi-circle of Isaias' remnant low and associated\ntornado potential will likely remain east of the TN River.\n\nRain and wind from Isaias' remnant low will exit to the northeast\nSunday morning. In its wake, an upstream ridge over TX and the\nwestern Gulf will amplify through the lower MS RIver valley\nthrough midweek. This ridge will likely reach 594dam at 500mb,\ncentered along the central Gulf coast. Midweek NBM max temps\nappear too cool for this pattern, likely due to seasonal bias\ncorrection.\n\nAlthough timing differences are evident in the 18Z global models,\nthis ridge is expected to lift to the mid-Atlantic seatboard late\nnext week, as a deep progressive longwave through lifts through\nthe Great Plains and MS River Valley.",
